General elections were held in [[Peru]] in on 9 April 2006 to elect the [[List of Presidents of Peru|President]], two Vice-Presidents, 120 members of [[Congress of Peru|Congress]] and five members of the [[Andean Parliament]] for the 2006–2011 period. As no presidential candidate received a majority of the vote, a second round was held on 4 June between the top two candidates, [[Ollanta Humala]] and [[Alan García]]. Garcia won the run-off with 52.63% to Humala's 47.37%. He was subsequently inaugurated on 28 July 2006, Peruvian Independence Day.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

==Electoral system==
The 120 members of Congress were elected from 25 constituencies based on the 24 [[Administrative divisions of Peru|departments]] and the [[Constitutional Province of Callao]]. The number of seats in Congress for each district was determined by its number of eligible voters. A political party need to win a minimum of five seats in two electoral districts or 4% of nationwide valid votes in order to be represented in Congress.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

A minimum of 4% of nationwide valid votes was necessary for a party to win seats in the Andean Parliament.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

