===Andean Parliament===
A total of 21 parties nominated 15 candidates for the Andean Parliament each, for a total of 315 candidates.  73 candidates were rejected by the National Jury of Elections, leaving 242 candidates from 19 parties. Participating parties include all those with Congressional candidates, except And It's Called Peru, Decentralization Coalition, Democratic Force, FREPAP and Let's Make Progress Peru.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

==Campaign==
===Presidential debate===
The only official presidential debate was held on May 21, 2006 between Ollanta Humala and Alan García, with journalist [[Augusto Álvarez Rodrich]] as moderator, in the National Museum of Archaeology. There were no debates before the first round.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

Humala arrived late, so García started the debate on his own, claiming that his opponent had "stopped at a bar for a sandwich" and accusing him of having "no respect for the country". Humala accused ''Aprista'' supporters of delaying his arrival.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

Álvarez Rodrich asked Humala to remove a small Peruvian flag on his podium before his first intervention, in order to have equitative images for both contenders. The candidate refused, saying that the national symbol was nothing to be ashamed about and arguing that the debate arrangements did not forbid using it, leading the moderator to withdraw the flag himself.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

The Union for Peru candidate attacked García's position on a bilateral free trade agreement with the United States as "ambiguous"; said that [[Vladimiro Montesinos]] would evidently vote for his opponent (since the former intelligence chief had  recently claimed that Humala's uprising in 2001 had been staged as a distraction for his escape from the country); reminded the audience of a tape showing Montesinos bribing former Peruvian Aprista Party Secretary-General [[Agustín Mantilla]]; alluded to a [[paramilitary]] group that operated during García's presidency; promised not to receive his salary if he got elected, but only his payments as a retired Lieutenant Colonel; proposed the formation of a Constituent Assembly to rewrite the Constitution on the basis of its 1979 version; and suggested the possibility of reopening a penitentiary, where corrupt government officials and "ex-presidents" would be sent, in a tacit attack at García.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

García called Humala a "demagogue" for promising to lower fuel prices by 30 percent; reminded his opponent of his earnings as a [[military attaché]] in [[France]] and [[South Korea]]; called on Humala for asking García to clarify whether he would free Montesinos or not, saying that such decision would concern the Judiciary branch anyway and that pretending to take such powers would be undemocratic, "in the style of (Hugo) Chávez"; indirectly pointed to Humala's support of his brother Antauro's 2005 rebellion, leading to the death of four policemen; and promised to enforce the payment of extra hours, stop arbitrary employment terminations and change some aspects of pension systems.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

The media and political analysts described the debate mostly as "boring" and centered on personal attacks, with García not delivering a decisive victory, despite his much greater political experience.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026942.php|title=Presidential Debate: Local Media Coverage|date=2006-05-22|last=Bazo|first=Fabiola|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060623091040/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026942.php|archive-date=2006-06-23|language=es}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026943.php|date=2006-05-22|title=Political Observers Assess the Presidential Debate|last=Bazo|first=Fabiola|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060623091054/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026943.php|archive-date=2006-06-23|language=es}}</ref> Opinion polls in [[Lima Metropolitan Area|Metropolitan Lima]] and [[Callao]] gave García a clear victory over Humala, though these were anti-Humala strongholds throughout the campaign.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/027179.php|title=IMA Lima& Callao Poll, May 22-24, 2006: Alan Garcia Winner of Presidential Debate|date=2006-05-26|last=Bazo|first=Fabiola|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070511230028/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/027179.php|archive-date=2007-05-11|language=es}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026975.php|title=APOYO Flash Poll, Metropolitan Lima: Garcia Won Presidential Debate|date=2006-05-23|last=Bazo|first=Fabiola|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071116180251/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026975.php|archive-date=2007-11-16|language=es}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026971.php|title=CPI Encuesta Flash sobre Debate Presidencial a nivel de Lima Metropolitana, 22 de mayo del 2006: Garcia claro ganador|date=2006-05-30|last=Bazo|first=Fabiola|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060909221601/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026971.php|archive-date=2006-09-09|language=es}}</ref> A debate between the technical teams of both candidates was held on May 28 in the Museum of the Nation.{{cn|date=June 2026}}

=== Highlights ===
* Important issues during the campaign included economic policy, unemployment, education, healthcare, drug trafficking, terrorist activities, the exploitation of the [[Camisea Gas Project|Camisea]] natural gas reserves, the management of ports by foreign companies, a maritime border dispute with [[Chile]], birth control and abortion, and renewal of Congress.{{cn|date=June 2026}}
* In January 2006, Ollanta Humala attended a ceremony held in honor of then President-Elect of [[Bolivia]], [[Evo Morales]], by [[Venezuela]]n President [[Hugo Chávez]], who publicly endorsed Humala.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/021585.php|title=Hugo Chavez and Evo Morales Eulogize Ollanta Humala|date=2006-01-03|last=Bazo|first=Fabiola|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060313235401/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/021585.php|archive-date=2006-03-13|language=es}}</ref> Chávez also called Lourdes Flores "the candidate of the Peruvian oligarchy".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/021893.php|title=Hugo Chavez calls Lourdes Flores "Candidate of the Oligarchy"|date=2006-01-10|last=Bazo|first=Fabiola|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060309203915/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/021893.php|archive-date=2006-03-09|language=es}}</ref> These events prompted a row between Chávez and the Peruvian government, who accused him of interfering in Peru's internal affairs. Humala later met with [[Brazil]]ian and [[Argentina|Argentine]] presidents [[Lula da Silva]] and [[Néstor Kirchner]]. The former had also invited Flores and García, but they declined.{{cn|date=June 2026}}
*In late April, after Chávez announced that Venezuela would leave the [[Andean Community]] unless [[Colombia]], [[Ecuador]] and Peru abandoned their plans for Free Trade Agreements with the [[United States]], Alan García criticized him, arguing that this was equivalent to blackmail, and that it was very hypocritical, considering the amounts of oil exports from Venezuela to the US. Chávez responded by calling García "corrupt" and a "thief", and the new candidate of Peru's rich and of the US, following Flores' defeat. He also endorsed Humala again and threatened to withdraw his ambassador to Peru if García won. After accusations by García and the Peruvian government of interference in the election by the Venezuelan president, Toledo and García were called "[[caiman]]s from the same well" and the former was labelled as US President [[George W. Bush|Bush]]'s "puppet" and "office boy" by Chávez. This led to the mutual withdrawal of ambassadors.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/2006_04_28.php|title=Alan Garcia in Dispute with Hugo Chavez|date=2006-05-04|last=Bazo Escudero|first=Jorge|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060504183430/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/2006_04_28.php|archive-date=2006-05-04|language=es}}</ref> García had been the notable exception to criticism of Humala's January meeting with Chávez, in an apparent move to avoid confrontation with the latter, who could be a leftist ally in the region during his potential presidency.{{cn|date=June 2026}}
* On May 25, a violent clash between supporters of Ollanta Humala and Alan García in [[Cusco]] left five injured people, including two (three by other accounts) wounded by gunshot.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/027144.php|title=Five Wounded in Battle Between APRA and UPP Militants in Cusco|date=2006-05-26|last=Cameron|first=Maxwell A.|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070511061357/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/027144.php|archive-date=2007-05-11|language=es}}</ref>
* National Unity was accused of buying off candidates to pull out of the race, including [[Fernando Olivera (politician)|Fernando Olivera]], who did withdraw, and Jaime Salinas. Olivera's FIM party later showed TV advertisements directly attacking Alan García, and was ordered to stop by the National Jury of Elections. The ruling was not obeyed and [[Jorge Del Castillo]], Secretary General of Peruvian Aprista Party and Member of Congress, showed documents allegedly proving a secret pact between FIM and Lourdes Flores' Unidad Nacional party accounting for the attacks on García. In response, Olivera sued him for forging the documents.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.elcomercioperu.com.pe/EdicionImpresa/Html/2006-04-08/impPolitica0485830.html|title=Olivera denuncia a Del Castillo por falsificación|date=2006-04-08|newspaper=El Comercio Peru|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060515042819/http://www.elcomercioperu.com.pe/EdicionImpresa/Html/2006-04-08/impPolitica0485830.html|archive-date=2006-05-15|language=es}}</ref>
* A section of National Unity accused the Peruvian Aprista Party of electoral fraud, following the narrow victory of Alan García over Lourdes Flores in the race for the second runoff spot.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026693.php|title=UN Accuses APRA of Fraud|date=2006-05-13|last=Cameron|first=Maxwell A.|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060617133839/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026693.php|archive-date=2006-06-17}}</ref>
* On April 7, just two days before the first round, José Cardó Guarderas (Democratic Reconstruction) and [[Ciro Gálvez]] (Andean Renaissance) dropped from the presidential race and announced their support for Lourdes Flores' candidacy and their opposition to Ollanta Humala's. Only Cardó presented his official resignation to the National Jury of Elections, but his votes were still counted officially. The day before, Ricardo Wong (And It's Called Peru), who had withdrawn his candidacy before and later retracted, announced that he dropped the race in favor of Alan García. Given the proximity of the election, there were no changes to the ballot.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.peru21.com/P21Online/Html/2006-04-07/olPolitica0485745.html|title=Cardó y Gálvez declinan candidaturas a favor de Flores Nano|publisher=Peru21|date=2006-04-12|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060412005040/http://www.peru21.com/P21Online/Html/2006-04-07/olPolitica0485745.html|archive-date=2006-04-12|language=es}}</ref>
* In the second round campaigning for the Peruvian elections [[Diego Maradona]] the Argentinian soccer star, announced that he would visit Peru on May 4 to play a friendly game with former Peruvian soccer players. Maradona has also expressed his support for Humala's campaign and is a personal friend of Cuban President [[Fidel Castro]] and Venezuelan President [[Hugo Chávez]]. In response to the announcement that Maradona was coming to Peru to support Humala the candidate for the APRA party Alan García was quoted as saying "Maradona comes by order from his friends in Cuba and Venezuela and even so Ollanta Humala will not manage to pull a goal on us". In the end, Maradona did not make any political statements after all.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ednoperu.com/noticia.php?IDnoticia=22160|title=Maradona jugaría por la campaña de Humala|date=2006|publisher=ednoperu|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080212125834/http://www.ednoperu.com/noticia.php?IDnoticia=22160|archive-date=2008-02-12|language=es}}</ref>
* Ollanta Humala was accused of torturing and killing peasants under the ''nom de guerre'' "Capitán Carlos" when he was the commander of a military base in the jungle from 1992 to 1993. National Unity was accused of buying off witnesses to testify against him. His brother Antauro confirmed that he used that name and said that he acted "according to the rules of the Army".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/023490.php|title=Antauro Humala says: Ollanta is "Capitán Carlos"|date=2006-02-28|last=Bazo Escudero|first=Jorge|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060405185630/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/023490.php|archive-date=2006-04-05|language=es}}</ref>
* In December 2005, Peru reached a deal with the [[United States]] on a [[Peru-United States Free Trade Agreement|Free Trade Agreement]] between the two countries. Some candidates, such as Ollanta Humala, opposed the agreement, others like Lourdes Flores supported it, and still others like Alan García approved parts of it and advocated revising it. The Toledo administration announced that it would be signed after the first round in order to avoid affecting the election's outcome.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.invertia.com/noticias/noticia.asp?idnoticia=1505766|title=TLC con EEUU enfrenta a candidatos a presidencia de Perú|date=2006-04-05|publisher=Invertia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080411051516/http://www.invertia.com/noticias/noticia.asp?idnoticia=1505766|archive-date=2008-04-11|language=es}}</ref> The agreement was finally signed on April 12 in [[Washington, D.C.]], and ratified by Peru's Congress on June 28, but must be ratified by the [[U.S. Congress]] before coming into force.{{cn|date=June 2026}}
* [[Isaac Humala]], father of candidates Ollanta and Ulises, said that he would free [[Shining Path]] and [[Túpac Amaru Revolutionary Movement|MRTA]] leaders [[Abimael Guzmán]] and [[Víctor Polay]], since he considered that terrorist movements no longer represent a threat to Peruvian society. This came after a letter was signed by several public figures, including Ulises and fellow candidates Javier Diez Canseco and [[Alberto Moreno (politician)|Alberto Moreno]], demanding a fair trial for Polay. Most candidates rushed to condemn Isaac Humala's comments, including Ollanta and even Ulises, whose candidacy had been openly supported by his father.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/024196.php|title=Isaac Humala and the Communiqué Requesting a Fair Sentence for Victor Polay|date=2006-03-19|last=Bazo|first=Fabiola|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060908172107/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/024196.php|archive-date=2006-09-08|language=es}}</ref> Around the same time, Elena Humala, the candidates' mother, claimed that homosexuals and rapists should be shot,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/024335.php|title=Updated - Elena Tasso de Humala, Mother of Candidate Ollanta Humala, calls for homosexuals to be shot|date=2006-03-23|last=Bazo|first=Fabiola|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060409082904/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/024335.php|archive-date=2006-04-09|language=es}}</ref> which apparently prompted Ollanta to ask his parents to stop making public statements.{{cn|date=June 2026}}
* In an April 4 interview with Argentine newspaper ''[[Página/12]]'', Ollanta Humala claimed that, if Lourdes Flores were elected, she would be overthrown in less than a year.<ref>{{cite web|last=Noriega|first=Carlos|url=http://www.pagina12.com.ar/diario/elmundo/4-65164-2006-04-04.html|title=“No soy de izquierda ni de derecha”|date=2006-04-04|publisher=Página 12|access-date=2026-06-27|language=es}}</ref> Given his past uprising, this was interpreted as a coup threat, leading opponents to accuse him again of having no respect for democracy.{{cn|date=June 2026}}
* Peru's only living Roman Catholic [[Cardinal (Catholicism)|Cardinal]], [[Juan Luis Cipriani]] exhorted the electorate not to fall for "messianic" candidates, in one of several tacit references to Ollanta Humala.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.peru21.com/P21Online/Html/2006-03-25/olPolitica0478508.html|title=Cardenal Cipriani advierte de "sobresaltos mesiánicos"|publisher=Peru21|date=2006-03-05|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927003906/http://www.peru21.com/P21Online/Html/2006-03-25/olPolitica0478508.html|archive-date=2007-09-27|language=es}}</ref>
* [[Minister of Justice]] Alejandro Tudela Chipotea announced that Antauro and Ollanta Humala would be sued for complicity with [[Vladimiro Montesinos]]. While their October 2000  uprising was publicized as an insurrection against the Fujimori administration, there have been accusations of it being staged in order to create a distraction so that the fugitive Montesinos could flee the country, which he did the same day aboard a yacht. César Mojovich, a former National Police Commissioner of [[Toquepala mine|Toquepala]], revealed this in a TV show, and apparently there are records of calls from Montesinos to the Humala brothers' military base just hours before the uprising.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.peru21.com/P21Online/Html/2006-04-07/Portada0485697.html|title=Ollanta y Antauro Humala serán denunciados por rebelión de Locumba|date=2006-04-07|publisher=Peru21|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927004132/http://www.peru21.com/P21Online/Html/2006-04-07/Portada0485697.html|archive-date=2007-09-27|language=es}}</ref> In mid-May 2006, Montesinos himself claimed that Humala's uprising was indeed a farce, though his statements were interpreted by some as an attempt to affect the outcome of the runoff election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026916.php|title=Vladimiro Montesinos Intervenes in Runoff Election|date=2006-05-20|last=Cameron|first=Maxwell A.|publisher=University of British Columbia|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060623234446/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/026916.php|archive-date=2006-06-23|language=es}}</ref>
* Union for Peru's vice-presidential candidate [[Carlos Alberto Torres Caro|Carlos Torres Caro]] was accused of sexual harassment by two of his former students, and of electoral fraud cover-up in 1995.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.peru.com/noticias/idocs/2006/1/31/detalledocumento_278048.asp|title=Lo acusaron de acoso sexual|publisher=Peru.com|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060218185035/http://www.peru.com/noticias/idocs/2006/1/31/detalledocumento_278048.asp|archive-date=2006-02-18|language=es}}</ref> The presidential candidate for Project Country, [[Marco Antonio Arrunátegui]], who withdrew from the race, had a pending judicial process for sexual exploitation of minors.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.criterios.com/modules.php?name=Noticias&file=article&sid=7326|title=Inician proceso a candidato presidencial peruano por proxenetismo|date=2006-01-30|publisher=Criterios|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927134553if_/http://www.criterios.com/modules.php?name=Noticias&file=article&sid=7326|archive-date=2007-09-27|language=es}}</ref>
* The National Electoral Jury admitted the failure of an Electoral Ethical Pact between the parties, given the widespread personal attacks between the candidates. Asociación Civil Transparencia, an unaffiliated citizen group, also lamented the overall tone of the campaign.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cpnradio.com.pe/html/2006/04/07/1/8768.htm|title=JNE admite que Pacto Etico Electoral no funcionó durante la campaña|publisher=CPN Radio|date=2006-04-07|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060527234950/http://www.cpnradio.com.pe/html/2006/04/07/1/8768.htm|archive-date=2006-05-27}}</ref>
* [[Organization of American States|OAS]] and [[European Parliament]] observers denied the possibility of an electoral fraud,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.peru21.com/P21Online/Html/2006-04-07/Portada0485705.html|title=Misión de la OEA y PE expresan confianza en transparencia de comicios|date=2006-04-07|publisher=Peru21|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060524055614/http://www.peru21.com/P21Online/Html/2006-04-07/Portada0485705.html|archive-date=2006-05-24|language=es}}</ref> which was suggested by Ollanta Humala, who accused the government of favoring Lourdes Flores in the first round. The OAS mission sent personnel to emergency zones in Peru, where remnants of [[Shining Path]] (''Sendero Luminoso'') were known to operate. SL called for a boycott of the election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/024103.php|title=Shining Path Calls for Boycott of Election|date=2006-03-16|publisher=University of British Columbia|last=Bazo Escudero|first=Jorge|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060523225952/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/024103.php|archive-date=2006-05-23|language=es}}</ref>
* The election was initially going to use two separate ballots, by decision of the National Office of Electoral Processes: one for the Presidential election, the other for the Congressional and Andean Parliament elections. After the formal protest of several parties, the National Jury of Elections ruled that the ballots had to be merged into one.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/023407.php|title=Headaches for Election Authorities|date=2006-02-26|publisher=University of British Columbia|last=Cameron|first=Maxwell A.|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060405185149/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/023407.php|archive-date=2006-04-05|language=es}}</ref>
* Members of the [[Military of Peru|Armed Forces]] and the National Police were able to vote for the first time in 180 years. Up to 50% were eligible to vote, with an estimated 35% actually voting.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/025176.php|title=35% of the Armed Forces Voted on Election Day|date=2006-04-12|publisher=University of British Columbia|last=Bazo Escudero|first=Jorge|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060418063357/http://weblogs.elearning.ubc.ca/peru/archives/025176.php|archive-date=2006-04-18|language=es}}</ref>

